WS73 development verification
=============================

Use a clean, committed x86-64 Linux kernel worktree with Clang/LLD, make,
Python 3 and QEMU. Reserve the shared machine budget before starting: the
builders below use four jobs, and KUnit uses a two-CPU hardware-free VM.
All outputs are under ../.dev/ws73-upstream, with a shared build.lock.
Never install these kernels/modules or run driver experiments on the host.

  python3 tools/testing/ws73/run-kunit.py --jobs 4
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/run-kunit.py --jobs 4 --core-only
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/run-kunit.py --jobs 4 --hci-only
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/run-kunit.py --jobs 4 --hci-only --kcsan
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/build-hci.py module
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/build-hci.py builtin

The default KUnit mode runs protocol, firmware, startup and shared endpoint
checks without binding the native HCI consumer. Runtime-link tests drive actual
startup/action and USB TX engines with explicit synthetic replies, exercise
gated publication and synchronous auxiliary probing, and separate local action
faults from shared transport faults. They do not execute real control URBs.
Core-only disables NET and
Bluetooth. HCI-only binds the production auxiliary/HCI consumer to synthetic backends.
It verifies failed enable, terminal rebind, two setup queries, packet ownership
and packet/byte queue bounds. It also checks ACL fragment copying at every
header/payload split, RX during a blocked active flush, queued-packet cancellation
and immediate TX rejection after malformed RX. ACL is inspected at the native
HCI receive queue; HCI_INIT drops it before L2CAP processing.
HCI-only enables FAILSLAB and uses the calling test task's fail_nth for one
allocation in TX or RX; it resets injection before assertions and verifies
actual consumption plus terminal ENOMEM. Worker tasks are not injected.
Four additional HCI cases compose the real runtime backend and native consumer:
startup/disable/open, USB wire sequencing and action replies, setup/ACL transport,
coalesced fragmented RX, short action/data completions and parent abort with TX
blocked. The model's I/O callbacks replace actual USB submissions; neither class
setup packets nor URBs are executed. The explicit test controller supplies
synthetic Features/BD_ADDR replies; production code never manufactures completions.
Reset is deliberately left unanswered, so full HCI initialization, Bluetooth
connections, BlueZ and peer behavior remain unqualified. Codec tests also run.
The checked-in KUnit configuration enables SMP, KASAN, UBSAN, lockdep and
DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP. Expected suite/case sets reject missing or skipped tests.

--kcsan replaces KASAN with strict KCSAN in the separate fixed
build-kunit-kcsan directory; UBSAN, SMP, lockdep and DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP remain.
It enables weak-memory modeling, early detection and boot selftests, with
KCSAN_SKIP_WATCH=1000. The runner requires the activation and 3/3 selftest
messages, the exact instrumentation configuration and compiler memory-access
hooks in shared endpoint/link objects (and native HCI in --hci-only mode).
Kernel diagnostics fail validation independently of KTAP PASS lines. Existing
concurrent HCI cases exercise blocked TX, flush, RX and abort against explicit
synthetic backends. KCSAN is sampled detection: a successful bounded run does
not prove absence of races, hardware behavior or all lifecycle interleavings.

The USB aggregate and H4 tests also run bounded deterministic mutation campaigns
with four fixed xorshift32 seeds each. USB supplies 16,384 unaligned inputs in
exact-sized allocations, including valid seeds, bit/length/table mutations,
truncation and extra bytes. It checks immutable input, no partial publication
on errors, and bounded contiguous views on success. H4 runs 16,384 steps mixing
fragments, malformed/random bytes and explicit resets. An independent byte
accumulator checks emitted packets against all consumed fragments, and failed
streams must reject further input without changing state until reset. Each
campaign must exercise acceptance/partial/error/terminal/reset paths as relevant;
the runner rejects missing, duplicate or malformed seed counters and wrong totals.
These are fixed-budget mutation checks of production functions in the VM, not
coverage-guided fuzzing, concurrent lifecycle stress, formal proof or hardware
qualification. State reset in this single-threaded test does not prove RX drain.

build-hci.py compiles/links the WS73 core, USB and HCI driver either built-in
or as modules, in the separate fixed build-hci directory. It builds bzImage
before modules so modpost has the matching kernel symbols; unresolved symbols
are errors. Configuration requests are checked explicitly, excluding ordinary
comments. Source, compiler candidates, configuration, build tool and output
hashes are recorded. Do not run with Python optimization. Compile/link success
does not prove module loading/unloading, suspend/resume or hardware behavior.

  python3 tools/testing/ws73/build-cross.py all

build-cross.py builds vmlinux and the actual shared/USB/native Bluetooth modules
with W=1 for arm64 and i386 using Clang, PowerPC64 big endian using Clang,
and x86-64 using GCC. Individual profile names are available through --help.
Run after checking the whole-machine build budget; profiles run sequentially
with four jobs and the shared build lock in separate fixed output directories.
It requires clean committed sources, checks requested Kconfig dependencies,
rejects test drivers and BROKEN, and verifies module/kernel ELF architecture,
word size and endianness. Each record retains logs, configuration, kernel,
module and tool hashes; failure records are retained too. No kernel/module is
installed or executed. This does not qualify cross-architecture runtime or
hardware behavior, module loading, Wi-Fi, or all built-in/module combinations.
The native Bluetooth stack has LE enabled and BR/EDR disabled explicitly;
the allnoconfig default would otherwise omit LE conditional compilation.
PowerPC64 supplies the big-endian build; arm64 big endian currently depends
on BROKEN and is deliberately excluded.

See usb-vm/README for boot/runtime and synthetic FILES/QUIT download integration
through dummy_hcd and FunctionFS, including actual 32-KiB URBs and READY wait.
The download phase uses synthetic payloads. The loader phase uses the local
original U/E files through the actual firmware API and complete boot sequence,
with a separate dummy_hcd-only ID. Neither qualifies physical board setup.
The physical USB parent remains observation-only: it does not
publish BLE until firmware, board, identity and startup gates are integrated
and qualified. Enabling BT_WS73 alone does not provide working physical BLE.
